  "id": "DrugBank_DB01114 - MONDO_grouped_2309_2308",
  "verbalization": "Chlorpheniramine, a first-generation antihistamine, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Papillary Conjunctivitis through a mechanism involving side effects that lead to erythroid hypoplasia and hypoplastic anemia. These conditions, in turn, are associated with Dexchlorpheniramine, a metabolite of Chlorpheniramine, which is indicated for Papillary Conjunctivitis. The evidence suggests a potential link between Chlorpheniramine and Papillary Conjunctivitis via its side effects, which may contribute to the pathophysiology of the condition. The shared pathway involves the metabolic transformation of Chlorpheniramine into Dexchlorpheniramine, which is linked to the disease through its indication. The final scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of support for this relationship, though the evidence is not definitive. The mechanism is supported by the presence of overlapping side effects and indications, suggesting a possible role of Chlorpheniramine in the development of Papillary Conjunctivitis through its metabolite and its associated side effects.",
